{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n`review_bot/adapter.py::_translate_findings` maps each finding's kernel severity\n(`critical/major/minor/none`) to the common vocabulary via its own copy of\n`_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY`, but nothing downstream ever renders that value to a human:\n`render_findings_block`/`_summarize` in `review_bot/finding_publish.py` render each finding as\n`- ({criterion}) {detail}[ {location}]` with NO severity word anywhere, and the Gerrit comment\nalready conveys blocking vs advisory structurally (via the batch each call renders: `blocking`\nitems under one header, `advisory` items under another). So the human-facing Gerrit comment\nALREADY satisfies \"blocking/advisory, never severity\" -- `finding_publish.py` needs NO change.\n\nThe real defect: `_translate_findings` flattens `verdict[\"blocking\"]` and `verdict[\"advisory\"]`\ninto ONE list with no field distinguishing which bucket a translated finding came from -- its\n`severity` computation is dead weight nothing reads. Separately, two CLI text renderers DO\nprint a severity word today, for findings that (after the sibling ticket\nbeaming-snively-urial) now also carry `priority`/`decision`: `_render_review_text`\n(`review_result` findings, from `shim.py`) prints `f.get('severity','?').upper()`;\n`_render_plan_review_text`'s advisory branch prints `sev={f.get('severity')}` (its blocking\nbranch already prints no severity). `_render_verdict_text` (completion_verdict findings) is\nOUT OF SCOPE: those findings come from `completion_reconcile.py`'s independent module cluster,\nnever pass through the review-kernel, and never carry `priority`/`decision` -- `severity` is\ntheir only signal and stays untouched.\n\n## Approach\n1. In `review_bot/adapter.py::_translate_findings`, replace the `severity` key with a\n   `blocking: bool` (`True` for items from `verdict[\"blocking\"]`, `False` for\n   `verdict[\"advisory\"]`) -- fixing the genuine gap that the flattened output cannot currently\n   tell the two buckets apart. Delete `_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY` from `adapter.py` (its only\n   use site).\n2. `review_bot/finding_publish.py` (`render_findings_block`/`post_review`) is NOT touched --\n   confirmed it renders no severity word today and already conveys blocking/advisory via its\n   `kind=` batching, satisfying the epic's human-facing design goal with zero code change.\n3. Update `_render_review_text` in `_cli/_llm_commands.py` to print `BLOCKING`/`ADVISORY`\n   (derived from the finding's `decision` field, which `beaming-snively-urial` already threads\n   onto `review_result` findings via `shim.py`) instead of `f.get('severity','?').upper()`.\n4. Update `_render_plan_review_text`'s advisory branch to drop the `sev={severity}` suffix\n   entirely (the function's blocking branch already omits any per-finding tag beyond its\n   `[BLOCK ...]`/`[advisory ...]` bucket label, so dropping the advisory branch's severity\n   suffix makes the two branches consistent -- no replacement tag needed since bucket\n   membership already conveys blocking/advisory).\n5. `_render_verdict_text` is explicitly OUT OF SCOPE (see Problem) -- left unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `grep -n \"_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY\" src/rebar/review_bot/adapter.py` returns no\n  matches; `grep -n \"_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY\" src/rebar/llm/code_review/shim.py` still\n  finds it (unchanged, out of scope for this ticket).\n- [ ] `adapter.py::_translate_findings`'s output carries a `blocking: bool` key per finding\n  instead of `severity`; `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_carry_forward.py -k test_adapter_carries_standing_through_to_the_receiver_shape`\n  passes, strengthened with an assertion that a `verdict[\"blocking\"]`-sourced finding has\n  `blocking=True` and a `verdict[\"advisory\"]`-sourced one has `blocking=False`.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/review_bot/finding_publish.py` has zero diff from this ticket\n  (`git diff --stat src/rebar/review_bot/finding_publish.py` is empty); `pytest\n  tests/unit/test_review_bot_finding_publish.py` passes unchanged.\n- [ ] `_render_review_text` prints `BLOCKING`/`ADVISORY` per finding instead of an uppercased\n  severity word; a strengthened or new test in the module covering it (locate via\n  `pytest --collect-only -q -k llm_commands`) asserts this.\n- [ ] `_render_plan_review_text`'s advisory branch no longer prints `sev=...`; the plan-review\n  CLI rendering tests (`tests/unit/test_plan_review_reuse.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py`, located via\n  `pytest --collect-only -q -k render_plan_review_text`) pass with updated assertions where\n  they touch the advisory rendering format.\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real `_translate_findings` and CLI renderer functions,\nfed production-shaped verdict/result dicts (contract test). Oracle: the exact\n`blocking`/`BLOCKING`/`ADVISORY` values and text, asserted directly; and a negative check that\n`finding_publish.py`'s rendered output is byte-identical to before (zero diff to that file is\nitself the contrast/regression check). Refactoring litmus: renaming an internal helper inside\na renderer must not break these tests, since they assert on the printed text/output value, not\non which helper produced it."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788133899094749099,"uuid":"5001d4fa-b8eb-4597-8b56-c7ec05554330"}